[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 395,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 228,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58252,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 228,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibroma of tendon sheath is a benign fibroblastic/myofibroblastic nodular proliferation, usually attached to a tendon (sheath).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 396,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 229,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58253,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 229,
        "headingText": "<p>8813/0 Fibroma of tendon sheath</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-31T09:23:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 397,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 230,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58254,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 230,
        "headingText": "<p>EE6Y &amp; XH0WB3 Other specified fibromatous disorders of skin and soft tissue &amp; Fibroma of tendon sheath</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-23T16:14:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 398,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 231,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58255,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 231,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> tenosynovial fibroma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 399,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 232,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58256,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58257,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The lesion typically occurs on the finger tendons. The thumb, index finger, and middle finger are the digits most frequently involved. Intra-articular locations (knee, elbow, wrist) have rarely been described {22028571; 21340629; 19644650}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58258,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumour presents as a firm, small (&le;&nbsp;3&nbsp;cm), slow-growing nodule.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58259,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibroma of tendon sheath is rare and typically occurs in patients aged 20&ndash;50&nbsp;years {2729499; 23212985}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58260,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58261,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A clonal chromosomal abnormality, t(2;11)(q31-q32;q12), has been described in two classic cases {9639118; 25202108}. Notably, an apparently identical translocation has also been observed in desmoplastic fibroblastoma, which can show morphological overlap with fibroma of tendon sheath {22411068; 15036892; 22137488}. Diffuse and strong nuclear FOSL1 expression seems to be characteristic of desmoplastic fibroblastoma and absent in fibroma of tendon sheath {27442992; 22411068}. Some cellular subtypes of fibroma of tendon sheath are characterized by <em>USP6</em> rearrangement, as seen in nodular fasciitis {27125357}. In view of the similar morphological and molecular genetic features, some lesions diagnosed as cellular fibroma of tendon sheath are probably in fact tenosynovial nodular fasciitis {16434899}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58262,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "<p>Fibroma of tendon sheath has a lobular fibrous appearance, reminiscent of a localized tenosynovial giant cell tumour, except for the pigment, which is absent in fibroma of tendon sheath.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-02-10T17:44:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58263,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The lesion is well circumscribed and contains bland spindle cells in a collagenous background. The cellularity is usually low but can be variable and is often higher at the tumour edge. There are characteristic slit-like thin-walled vessels. Degenerative features such as myxoid/cystic changes, chondroid or osseous metaplasia, and bizarre pleomorphic cells can be seen. The morphological features of the cellular subtype are identical to those of nodular fasciitis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58264,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 240,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58265,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 240,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 410,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 243,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58266,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 243,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> benign firm nodule most often on the finger tendons; usually a paucicellular collagen-rich lesion with slit-like vessels.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 408,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 241,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58267,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 241,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 409,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 242,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58268,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 27,
        "headingId": 242,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibroma of tendon sheath is benign but can recur in 5&ndash;10% of cases.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:17:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]